      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "The line $y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 1$ is perpendicular to which line?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$y = \\frac{2}{3}x + 4$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$y = -\\frac{3}{2}x + 2$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$y = \\frac{3}{2}x - 5$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 7$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "c",
      "explanation": "The gradient of the given line is $-\\frac{2}{3}$. The perpendicular gradient is $-\\frac{1}{-2/3} = \\frac{3}{2}$. The line $y = \\frac{3}{2}x - 5$ has this gradient."
